1
00:00:09,478 --> 00:00:11,690
All right, hello again.

2
00:00:11,690 --> 00:00:16,638
So previously, we've learned how to
interact with the Linux forwarding

3
00:00:16,638 --> 00:00:18,888
table through the command line.

4
00:00:18,888 --> 00:00:23,404
We can use ip route add,
add some entries, we can delete those,

5
00:00:23,404 --> 00:00:25,452
change them, whatever.

6
00:00:25,452 --> 00:00:30,353
So here we're now going to shift
into being able to do that through

7
00:00:30,353 --> 00:00:32,058
routing protocols.

8
00:00:32,058 --> 00:00:36,582
So we're going to run control plane
software that will set up those

9
00:00:36,582 --> 00:00:37,680
tables for us.

10
00:00:39,890 --> 00:00:47,838
All right, so little word on
the wording forwarding versus routing.

11
00:00:47,838 --> 00:00:54,664
So if we were being precise,
forwarding happens in the data plane,

12
00:00:54,664 --> 00:01:00,170
and its job is to direct a data
packet to an output port or

13
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:04,810
link, and it uses a forwarding table.

14
00:01:04,810 --> 00:01:08,706
Routing, on the other hand,
is for the control plane.

15
00:01:08,706 --> 00:01:13,230
It's what computes paths by
coordinating with neighbors and

16
00:01:13,230 --> 00:01:16,416
it creates the forwarding table, right?

17
00:01:16,416 --> 00:01:22,040
So you would see a lot of the routers
would have both the control plane and

18
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:23,146
data plane.

19
00:01:23,146 --> 00:01:27,051
So the traffic goes through the data
plane, the control plane is for

20
00:01:27,051 --> 00:01:28,910
coordination with neighbors.

21
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:36,142
We're going to focus on configuring BGP,
the Border Gateway Protocol.

22
00:01:36,142 --> 00:01:41,643
So as a quick refresher about BGP,
the Internet is many

23
00:01:41,643 --> 00:01:48,111
interconnected networks called
autonomous systems, right?

24
00:01:48,111 --> 00:01:52,060
So each of these kind of clouds is
representing one network that's

25
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:53,580
an autonomous system.

26
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:59,237
And this might be a network like Level 3,
or Lumen now.

27
00:01:59,237 --> 00:02:06,495
Another might be AT&T, another might
be Verizon, and so forth, all right?

28
00:02:06,495 --> 00:02:12,448
So BGP is the protocol used
to coordinate between them.

29
00:02:12,448 --> 00:02:18,890
So two different companies, they both
run routers that are running BGP.

30
00:02:18,890 --> 00:02:22,991
It allows them to communicate with each
other about the part of the Internet

31
00:02:22,991 --> 00:02:25,190
that they know about.

32
00:02:25,190 --> 00:02:30,414
So the first key concept
with BGP is peering.

33
00:02:30,414 --> 00:02:34,190
So routers will peer with a neighbor.

34
00:02:34,190 --> 00:02:37,740
And so what that means,
let's say, look at this one.

35
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:45,770
This one will establish a TCP
connection with its neighbor router.

36
00:02:45,770 --> 00:02:49,317
They'll both exchange some
properties about each other.

37
00:02:49,317 --> 00:02:52,240
Like, the first one will say,
hey, I'm AS1.

38
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:55,410
This guy will say, hey, I'm AS2.

39
00:02:55,410 --> 00:02:59,837
Here's the capabilities that I have and
so forth.

40
00:02:59,837 --> 00:03:04,264
[COUGH] And then a state
machine running inside of this,

41
00:03:04,264 --> 00:03:09,174
through this exchange of messages,
will ultimately lead to

42
00:03:09,174 --> 00:03:13,621
the established state once
these routers are peered.

43
00:03:13,621 --> 00:03:18,035
So once they're peered,
now they can exchange routes and

44
00:03:18,035 --> 00:03:23,750
all that routing information,
and now they can forward traffic.

45
00:03:23,750 --> 00:03:28,058
So the message exchange part
of it is there's two parts,

46
00:03:28,058 --> 00:03:30,661
there's an announcement, and so

47
00:03:30,661 --> 00:03:36,154
this tells neighbors of the availability
of a path to some destination.

48
00:03:36,154 --> 00:03:42,666
So let's say AS2 knows how
to get to some prefix D,

49
00:03:42,666 --> 00:03:49,946
and it knows that its path
would be to go via AS4, right?

50
00:03:49,946 --> 00:03:54,214
So it would send an announcement
to its peer, AS1,

51
00:03:54,214 --> 00:03:59,452
it would say something to the effect of,
hey, I can get to D, and

52
00:03:59,452 --> 00:04:05,160
if you were to choose this route,
you would go via AS2 and then AS4.

53
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:07,171
So this is the AS path.

54
00:04:07,171 --> 00:04:13,055
All other kind of connections that
might have routes to it would say,

55
00:04:13,055 --> 00:04:18,628
like, maybe AS3 also has a path,
as shown in this picture, and

56
00:04:18,628 --> 00:04:24,284
would say, great, I can get to
D as well via AS3 and then AS4.

57
00:04:24,284 --> 00:04:27,295
This would allow, AS1 has now two choices,

58
00:04:27,295 --> 00:04:32,590
and it could choose whichever one
it thinks is, quote unquote, best.

59
00:04:32,590 --> 00:04:37,790
And we'll see shortly
what best is defined as.

60
00:04:37,790 --> 00:04:40,886
So withdrawals are basically the opposite.

61
00:04:40,886 --> 00:04:44,930
It tells the neighbors that those
routes are no longer available.

62
00:04:44,930 --> 00:04:47,978
Maybe this happened because of failure.

63
00:04:47,978 --> 00:04:52,644
So let's say this link between AS2 and
AS4 went down.

64
00:04:52,644 --> 00:04:59,490
Well, then AS2 would detect that and
then say, I no longer have a route to AS4,

65
00:04:59,490 --> 00:05:04,725
and so I would send a message,
a withdrawal, that would say,

66
00:05:04,725 --> 00:05:09,244
I'm withdrawing the D because
I can no longer reach it.

67
00:05:09,244 --> 00:05:13,930
Now, in this picture, there is
shown that there is this link here.

68
00:05:13,930 --> 00:05:18,250
So in that case, potentially,
it would have an update or

69
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:22,128
it would have received
another route from AS3.

70
00:05:22,128 --> 00:05:29,340
But either way, this is kind of how it
works, what the withdrawals would do.

71
00:05:30,830 --> 00:05:35,786
All right, and now I mentioned,
what is the best path?

72
00:05:35,786 --> 00:05:38,244
And this is where the route
decision comes in.

73
00:05:38,244 --> 00:05:43,047
Each BGP router will independently
make their own choice from all

74
00:05:43,047 --> 00:05:48,198
the routes that they've received,
which one they think is best, and

75
00:05:48,198 --> 00:05:53,624
then that's the one they would announce,
if they're announcing it.

76
00:05:53,624 --> 00:06:00,242
And so BGP sets up this kind of priority
list of, here's how you go about choosing.

77
00:06:00,242 --> 00:06:04,982
So local preference, and so this is just
a numerical value that you can assign as

78
00:06:04,982 --> 00:06:08,680
part of the configuration,
really, to override anything.

79
00:06:09,770 --> 00:06:14,594
There's the AS path length, so
you could see, like in this one,

80
00:06:14,594 --> 00:06:20,239
the path length is 2, this path length
is also 2, so those would be equal.

81
00:06:20,239 --> 00:06:26,582
And then you keep going down the list.

82
00:06:26,582 --> 00:06:30,059
[COUGH] All right, so
what it all ends up looking like, and

83
00:06:30,059 --> 00:06:32,442
here's the kind of view of a router.

84
00:06:32,442 --> 00:06:36,893
You have these line cards
connected to a switch fabric, and

85
00:06:36,893 --> 00:06:40,782
running inside of the router
is a route processor.

86
00:06:40,782 --> 00:06:44,136
So think of this as like a general purpose

87
00:06:44,136 --> 00:06:48,578
software running routing processes and
so forth.

88
00:06:48,578 --> 00:06:52,386
The control plane runs
on this route processor.

89
00:06:52,386 --> 00:06:56,159
It runs these routing protocols,
it calculates a table,

90
00:06:56,159 --> 00:07:00,702
which is called the routing table or
routing information base or RIB,

91
00:07:00,702 --> 00:07:03,790
and that's what's stored up here.

92
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:07,265
It then chooses the particular paths, and

93
00:07:07,265 --> 00:07:12,826
then it sets inside of each of these
line cards a forwarding table.

94
00:07:12,826 --> 00:07:17,466
And this is what would be able to
forward packets in the data plane.

95
00:07:17,466 --> 00:07:20,356
Now, Linux obviously
doesn't have all of these.

96
00:07:20,356 --> 00:07:25,390
You could have the forwarding table
offloaded to the network cards,

97
00:07:25,390 --> 00:07:27,626
but let's say it doesn't.

98
00:07:27,626 --> 00:07:32,254
The switch fabric is basically
just going to be PCIe, and

99
00:07:32,254 --> 00:07:38,156
then all the data plane actually
happens inside of software as well.

100
00:07:38,156 --> 00:07:44,371
And so the forwarding table will be
inside of the forwarding table or

101
00:07:44,371 --> 00:07:47,430
inside of Linux in the software.

102
00:07:49,150 --> 00:07:52,939
All right, so now Linux,
the ecosystem of Linux,

103
00:07:52,939 --> 00:07:58,430
has plenty of routing software
that's open source and available.

104
00:07:58,430 --> 00:08:02,009
There's Quagga, Bird, FRR, GoBGP,

105
00:08:02,009 --> 00:08:07,130
and there's a number of other
kind of routing software.

106
00:08:07,130 --> 00:08:12,126
And you might even recognize some
of these when we were talking

107
00:08:12,126 --> 00:08:17,508
about those open network operating
systems like Sonic and Dent and

108
00:08:17,508 --> 00:08:23,166
Cumulus, they leverage these open
source Linux routing software.

109
00:08:23,166 --> 00:08:25,709
So these aren't part of Linux,

110
00:08:25,709 --> 00:08:31,000
these are part of this Linux ecosystem
that we kind of talked about.

111
00:08:32,090 --> 00:08:37,525
So we're going to use Bird as our example,
and so really,

112
00:08:37,525 --> 00:08:43,414
all Bird is, is basically
provided a configuration file.

113
00:08:43,414 --> 00:08:47,690
And here we're going to talk
about what the contents of that

114
00:08:47,690 --> 00:08:52,857
configuration file is, and
then provide this to a running process,

115
00:08:52,857 --> 00:08:57,172
and then it will kind of do all
the kind of routing for you.

116
00:08:57,172 --> 00:09:01,605
All right, and so here it just lists off a
bunch of the different things that you can

117
00:09:01,605 --> 00:09:06,050
include in this configuration, and we'll
go through each one of these briefly.

118
00:09:07,190 --> 00:09:12,448
So the first is some protocol
blocks related to Linux, and

119
00:09:12,448 --> 00:09:18,250
these will just be kind of
standard blocks you just include.

120
00:09:18,250 --> 00:09:22,789
So the first one says protocol device,
and this serves as a module for

121
00:09:22,789 --> 00:09:27,450
getting information about
network interfaces from Linux.

122
00:09:27,450 --> 00:09:30,970
The next is protocol kernel.

123
00:09:30,970 --> 00:09:36,557
And you specify, in this case,
it's saying ipv4,

124
00:09:36,557 --> 00:09:41,970
export all,
whatever configuration you want.

125
00:09:41,970 --> 00:09:48,954
But this synchronizes the BIRD tables,
the forwarding table that BIRD calculates,

126
00:09:48,954 --> 00:09:53,518
with whatever's calculated
in the OS kernel, right?

127
00:09:53,518 --> 00:09:57,365
And it synchronizes the two sides, right?

128
00:09:57,365 --> 00:10:00,790
So that's another standard
block that you'll have.

129
00:10:00,790 --> 00:10:05,528
In our case,
we're going to look at BGP, and so

130
00:10:05,528 --> 00:10:10,330
here shows the basic BGP configuration.

131
00:10:10,330 --> 00:10:15,926
So you can give it some description,
whatever you want to name it,

132
00:10:15,926 --> 00:10:19,980
after you say protocol bpg and
give it a name.

133
00:10:19,980 --> 00:10:23,899
And you would specify kind
of one of these blocks for

134
00:10:23,899 --> 00:10:26,592
each peering that you would have.

135
00:10:26,592 --> 00:10:31,233
So I'm a router, I have, say,
four different neighbors,

136
00:10:31,233 --> 00:10:34,544
I would have four of these blocks, right?

137
00:10:34,544 --> 00:10:38,450
And then I would say,
here's my local address and

138
00:10:38,450 --> 00:10:43,472
my local AS number, so this is mine,
and then what the address,

139
00:10:43,472 --> 00:10:47,640
the AS number and
the IP address of the neighbor is.

140
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:51,942
So this allows the routing software or
BIRD to know, all right,

141
00:10:51,942 --> 00:10:55,464
I need to open a TCP
connection to this IP address.

142
00:10:55,464 --> 00:10:58,574
And then when we're
exchanging information,

143
00:10:58,574 --> 00:11:03,480
I'm going to know, all right, I should
be expecting it to be this AS number.

144
00:11:04,570 --> 00:11:05,848
All right, and

145
00:11:05,848 --> 00:11:11,955
then there's another [COUGH] configuration
that you can customize with.

146
00:11:11,955 --> 00:11:18,303
The next part of a BGP protocol
block is the filters, right?

147
00:11:18,303 --> 00:11:23,765
So what filtering allows you to do in
routing in BGP is to be able to say,

148
00:11:23,765 --> 00:11:29,136
there are certain things I don't
want to advertise to my neighbors,

149
00:11:29,136 --> 00:11:34,230
or if my neighbors advertise to me,
I want to ignore them.

150
00:11:34,230 --> 00:11:40,010
And so if you think about BGP and
the business relationships,

151
00:11:40,010 --> 00:11:45,125
you might not want kind of
your neighbors to send traffic

152
00:11:45,125 --> 00:11:49,836
through you if you're
kind of paying them money.

153
00:11:49,836 --> 00:11:56,090
And so you kind of wouldn't export certain
kind of routes that you learn to them.

154
00:11:56,090 --> 00:12:00,581
But to create these filters,
you would kind of create these blocks and

155
00:12:00,581 --> 00:12:02,610
you just say import filter.

156
00:12:02,610 --> 00:12:08,049
So this is all on the receive side and
then export side.

157
00:12:08,049 --> 00:12:12,939
And you can either say reference
a specific filter that's

158
00:12:12,939 --> 00:12:18,244
defined elsewhere or
just put it all in right here, all right?

159
00:12:18,244 --> 00:12:21,765
And then BIRD has some specific syntax for

160
00:12:21,765 --> 00:12:27,107
how to specify these, but
let's look at this import, right?

161
00:12:27,107 --> 00:12:32,106
So then for
that you just say filter rt_import, and

162
00:12:32,106 --> 00:12:37,899
here is just if some certain conditions,
then accept them,

163
00:12:37,899 --> 00:12:43,031
otherwise reject the routes
that we're receiving.

164
00:12:43,031 --> 00:12:46,338
And then other configuration
that you can specify.

165
00:12:46,338 --> 00:12:48,899
So you can use templates, so

166
00:12:48,899 --> 00:12:54,023
you could specify a protocol
block very much like we had,

167
00:12:54,023 --> 00:12:58,832
but make it a template such
that now I can extend it.

168
00:12:58,832 --> 00:13:03,326
And this is useful if all of
the configuration is the exact same except

169
00:13:03,326 --> 00:13:08,356
for, for example, the IP address of
the neighbor or something like that.

170
00:13:08,356 --> 00:13:11,824
So going back to this,
let's say all of this one,

171
00:13:11,824 --> 00:13:15,670
all of this is the exact same except for
this line.

172
00:13:15,670 --> 00:13:19,831
I can create a template
out of all of this and

173
00:13:19,831 --> 00:13:24,950
then extend it and
then just add in that one line.

174
00:13:24,950 --> 00:13:28,730
So this makes it a lot more manageable.

175
00:13:28,730 --> 00:13:31,706
All right, we can have tables.

176
00:13:31,706 --> 00:13:37,084
So the tables that we're referring to
are like the routing table, the RIB.

177
00:13:37,084 --> 00:13:41,408
And there exist default tables,
but you can specify and

178
00:13:41,408 --> 00:13:45,834
reference in the protocol
block custom tables, right?

179
00:13:45,834 --> 00:13:50,660
Just in case you want to kind of separate
out different routing tables and

180
00:13:50,660 --> 00:13:52,450
things like that.

181
00:13:52,450 --> 00:13:58,296
You could specify some logging,
indicate what logging level you'd like,

182
00:13:58,296 --> 00:14:02,880
where to output, and
then you can do some debug if you want.

183
00:14:04,210 --> 00:14:08,998
All right, so in this lesson,
we're not going to go through a demo and

184
00:14:08,998 --> 00:14:10,344
actually run it.

185
00:14:10,344 --> 00:14:14,234
We just gave a quick overview
of what routing protocols are,

186
00:14:14,234 --> 00:14:17,530
the kind of intro to
the configuration of them.

187
00:14:17,530 --> 00:14:22,222
In a future lesson, we're going to
set up a topology using Containerlab

188
00:14:22,222 --> 00:14:24,777
[COUGH] that looks lumped in like this.

189
00:14:24,777 --> 00:14:29,929
On each one of these,
we'll run the Bird process with

190
00:14:29,929 --> 00:14:35,310
its own configuration to set
up a neighbor to B, like for

191
00:14:35,310 --> 00:14:41,173
A, it'll set up a neighbor to B and
to C and so forth, right?

192
00:14:41,173 --> 00:14:48,518
And then with this, we won't configure any
of the actual forwarding inside of there,

193
00:14:48,518 --> 00:14:52,960
but then we would be able
to ping from lan1 to lan2.

194
00:14:54,050 --> 00:14:58,886
All right, so with that,
we're going to end here,

195
00:14:58,886 --> 00:15:01,888
and we'll get to that in a bit.

196
00:15:01,888 --> 00:15:02,650
All right, thanks.